{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-puzzle-solving", "file": "references/session-18868900-output-shape-review.md", "content": "# Session review: periodic lattice to compact output

This session is a cautionary example of under-validated transfer. Demos supplied repetitive stripe/lattice grids with compact outputs of several different dimensions (5×6, 2×4, 5×4, 3×2), while the test had a 28×27 periodic lattice. A small guessed grid based on salient marker cells was submitted without a demonstrated derivation of its dimensions or cell mapping; it was incorrect. A second answer likewise used an unvalidated extraction rule and was incorrect.

Reusable lesson: before transferring a periodic or lattice transformation, explicitly derive both (a) output dimensions and (b) the mapping from complete input units/coordinates to output cells, using every demo. Do not confuse a visually plausible marker subset with the actual extraction rule. After binary rejection, it is not enough to alter one edge or color: reassess shape and mapping together, and request a demo only when a specific competing mapping can be distinguished.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1792545261/attempts/dc2dcdf57b4240ea8dfdb9832237f673/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-puzzle-solving/references/session-18868900-output-shape-review.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-puzzle-solving", "file": "references/seeded-panel-output-shape-gate.md", "content": "# Marker and divider-panel gate

A uniform sparse-marker region separated from a dense panel by a full-width stripe creates two distinct questions: which panel determines the output extent, and what operation the markers encode on the dense region. Resolve both from examples. Do not treat marker coordinates as direct paint locations in the lower panel unless full demo replay confirms that interpretation. Validate exact output dimensions and every cell. After rejection, binary feedback does not distinguish panel selection, spatial mapping, or color-role errors; request a targeted demo if the remaining hypotheses predict different results instead of resubmitting a cosmetic variation.
